-- The remote call is exactly what Cobalt captured:
--   MachineCraftRequested:FireServer({ PetIds = { 6 x uuid }, Machine = "Golden", Action = "Craft" })
-- The old code only GUESSED how the pets are stored in the player data (field names), so it picked wrong pets or none.
-- Now: (1) a __namecall hook watches MachineCraftRequested; when you craft ONCE by hand it snapshots the pets you used,
-- finds the pet table in the replica data and learns which fields make a "same pet" group + how many pets are needed;
-- (2) without a learned craft it falls back to a generic scan of the data; (3) every craft is verified (pets must disappear),
-- a rejected set is blacklisted for a while; (4) "Dump pet data" prints what the script sees so the layout can be fixed fast.
-- Diamond / Rainbow: craft one by hand and the hook learns them the same way (their own remote/args are not assumed).
